{"id":"containers","corpus":"k8s","title":"Containers","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/containers/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Containers"},{"kind":"p","text":"Technology for packaging an application along with its runtime dependencies."},{"kind":"p","text":"This page will discuss containers and container images, as well as their use in operations and solution development."},{"kind":"p","text":"The word container is an overloaded term. Whenever you use the word, check whether your audience uses the same definition."},{"kind":"p","text":"Each container that you run is repeatable; the standardization from having dependencies included means that you get the same behavior wherever you run it."},{"kind":"p","text":"Containers decouple applications from the underlying host infrastructure. This makes deployment easier in different cloud or OS environments."},{"kind":"p","text":"Each node in a Kubernetes cluster runs the containers that form the Pods assigned to that node. Containers in a Pod are co-located and co-scheduled to run on the same node."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Container images"},{"kind":"p","text":"A container image is a ready-to-run software package containing everything needed to run an application: the code and any runtime it requires, application and system libraries, and default values for any essential settings."},{"kind":"p","text":"Containers are intended to be stateless and immutable: you should not change the code of a container that is already running. If you have a containerized application and want to make changes, the correct process is to build a new image that includes the change, then recreate the container to start from the updated image."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Container runtimes"},{"kind":"p","text":"A fundamental component that empowers Kubernetes to run containers effectively. It is responsible for managing the execution and lifecycle of containers within the Kubernetes environment."},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes supports container runtimes such as containerd, CRI-O, and any other implementation of the Kubernetes CRI (Container Runtime Interface)."},{"kind":"p","text":"Usually, you can allow your cluster to pick the default container runtime for a Pod. If you need to use more than one container runtime in your cluster, you can specify the RuntimeClass for a Pod to make sure that Kubernetes runs those containers using a particular container runtime."},{"kind":"p","text":"You can also use RuntimeClass to run different Pods with the same container runtime but with different settings."}],"lemmas":{"containers":["container","containers"],"technologies":["technology","technologies"],"packaging":["packaging","package"],"packagings":["packaging","packagings"],"packages":["package","packages"],"packaged":["packaged","package"],"alongs":["alongs","along"],"its":["its","it"],"dependencies":["dependency","dependencies"],"pages":["page","pages"],"paging":["paging","page"],"wills":["will","wills"],"willing":["willing","will"],"willed":["willed","will"],"would":["would","will"],"discuss":["discus","discuss"],"discuses":["discus","discuses"],"discusses":["discusses","discuss"],"discussed":["discussed","discuss"],"discussing":["discussing","discuss"],"images":["image","images"],"imaged":["imaged","image"],"imaging":["imaging","image"],"used":["used","use"],"operations":["operation","operations"],"solutions":["solution","solutions"],"developments":["development","developments"],"words":["word","words"],"wording":["wording","word"],"worded":["worded","word"],"overloaded":["overloaded","overload"],"overloadeds":["overloadeds","overloaded"],"overloads":["overload","overloads"],"terms":["term","terms"],"termed":["termed","term"],"terming":["terming","term"],"whenevers":["whenevers","whenever"],"checks":["check","checks"],"checking":["checking","check"],"checked":